Cell[TextData[{
 "Algebra with this product alone makes so called ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       SubscriptBox[
        StyleBox["K", "TI"], 
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox["p", "TI"], ",", 
          StyleBox["q", "TI"]}]], TraditionalForm], "errors" -> {}, "input" -> 
     "K_{p,q}", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "13cd7051-3c8b-46d8-9a34-f3c9fdf618c7"],
 " algebras. These algebras also are called algebras of multiple imaginary \
units, where each basis vector represents different imaginary unit. In \
Clifford algebra, however, we can introduce a new commutative product and \
define a \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]field\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] with multiple \
commutative units as (same or distinct) Clifford algebra. The construction, \
therefore, will result into Clifford algebra over field with multiple \
imaginary units. "
}], "Text",
